### Domain validators (pure)

Need careful algorithms.

`vehicleYearBounds(today: Date = new Date())`:
```ts
const currentYear = today.getFullYear();
return { min: currentYear - 20, max: currentYear + 1 } as const;
```
Return readonly object.

`isLegalAge(birthDateIso: string, today: Date = new Date())`:
- parse strict `YYYY-MM-DD` using regex + Date.UTC or local date components. Need reject impossible dates (2026-02-30). Use:
```ts
const match = /^(\d{4})-(\d{2})-(\d{2})$/.exec(birthDateIso.trim());
if (!match) return false;
const year=Number(match[1]) etc.
if (year < 1900 || month <1 || month>12 || day<1) return false;
const parsed = new Date(year, month - 1, day);
if (parsed.getFullYear() !== year || parsed.getMonth() !== month-1 || parsed.getDate() !== day) return false; // handles bad dates, but note years 0-99 map to 1900+year; year>=1900 so fine
const todayLocal = new Date(today.getFullYear(), today.getMonth(), today.getDate());
if (parsed > todayLocal) return false;
const turns18Year = year + 18;
let anniversaryMonth = month;
let anniversaryDay = day;
if (month === 2 && day === 29 && !isLeapYear(turns18Year)) anniversaryDay = 28;
const turns18 = new Date(turns18Year, anniversaryMonth - 1, anniversaryDay);
return turns18 <= todayLocal;
```
Also ensure birthDate >= 1900-01-01. Good. If today before 1900? not relevant.

`isValidClRut(value: string)`:
- Accept dotted/dashed/bare: strip `.` and `-`, trim, uppercase. Format: `^(\d{1,3}(?:\d{3})*|(\d+))-([0-9K])$`? Need handle bare 8 digits + check digit; normalized canonical `12345678-9`. Common RUT bodies 7-8 digits, but allow 1-9? Use `/^(\d{7,8})([0-9K])$/` after removing separators. Wait spec examples: `12.345.678-9` → body 12345678 (8 digits), check 9. `12.345.6789` (no dash) → body 12345678, check 9. Chilean RUT can be 7 digits too (e.g., 1.234.567-8). Use `/^(\d{7,8})([0-9K])$/`. Dotted group validation: dotted input should have proper grouping? Accept dotted/dashed/bare, can simply remove dots/dashes then regex. But `12.34.567-8` would be accepted; spec doesn't require strict grouping. Fine. Could reject if body length <7 or >8. Also check leading zero body maybe allowed? no need.
- Módulo 11:
```
let sum=0, multiplier=2;
for (let i = body.length -1; i>=0; i--) { sum += Number(body[i]) * multiplier; multiplier = multiplier === 7 ? 2 : multiplier+1; }
const result = 11 - (sum % 11);
const expected = result === 11 ? '0' : result === 10 ? 'K' : String(result);
return check === expected;
```
`normalizeClRut(value: string): string`:
- strip non `[0-9kK]`? But if invalid characters, return cleaned string? Spec: normalizes dotted/dashed/bare to `12345678-9` uppercase K. Implementation: `const cleaned = value.replace(/[.\-\s]/g, '').toUpperCase(); const match = /^(\d{7,8})([0-9K])$/.exec(cleaned); if (!match) return cleaned; return \`${match[1]}-${match[2]}\`;` That returns e.g. `12345678-K`. Good. Tests may call `normalizeClRut('12.345.678-5')` expecting `12345678-5`. Works. If input `abc` returns `ABC`; harmless.
- Note fixture `12.345.678-5` valid? Need verify check digit 5 for body 12345678; yes likely.

`isValidCoDocument(documentType: string, value: string)`:
```
const digits = value.trim();
if (!/^[0-9]+$/.test(digits)) return false;
const normalizedType = documentType.trim().toUpperCase();
if (normalizedType === 'CC') return len>=6 && len<=10;
if (normalizedType === 'CE') return len>=6 && len<=12;
return false;
```

`normalizePlate(value: string): string` => `value.trim().toUpperCase()`.

Also add `isValidClPlate`? Domain rule `clPlate`: pattern handles. Could add helper `isValidPlate(value: string, country: CountryCode): boolean` for validator. Not required but useful. Maybe export `isValidCoPlate`/`isValidClPlate` for tests? User only lists normalizePlate. Keep pattern-based validators in factory using `Validators.pattern` plus domain rule wrappers that call `normalizePlate` and regex? Since seed has pattern, `Validators.pattern` is enough; still add `clPlateValidator` to check normalized value against pattern after uppercase? Angular input value may be lowercase; pattern is uppercase, so blur transforms to uppercase, but user can type lowercase and attempt submit without blur? Blur happens when clicking submit button, so okay. But to be robust, validator can normalize for plate rules. Implement in factory: for `coPlate`/`clPlate`, apply `Validators.pattern` from config and a custom validator that checks uppercase-normalized value against pattern. Need avoid double errors irrelevant since single message. Fine.

Also minDate/maxDate validators for date fields. Add `dateBoundsValidator(minDate, maxDate)`.

### CatalogueApi

```ts
@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })
export class CatalogueApi {
  private readonly http = inject(HttpClient);
  private readonly apiBaseUrl = inject(API_BASE_URL);

  getFormFields(country: CountryCode): Observable<readonly FormFieldConfig[]> {
    const url = `${this.apiBaseUrl}/api/form-fields?filters[country][$eq]=${country}&sort=order:asc&pagination[pageSize]=100`;
    return this.http.get<StrapiListResponse<FormFieldDto>>(url).pipe(
      map((response) => this.mapFields(response, country))
    );
  }
```
Exact string. Need encode? Use raw as spec exact. Country restricted type so safe.

Mapping:
```ts
private mapFields(response, country): readonly FormFieldConfig[] {
 const rows = Array.isArray(response.data) ? response.data : [];
 if (response.meta?.pagination && response.meta.pagination.total > rows.length && isDevMode()) console.warn(...)
 const seen = new Set<string>();
 const mapped: FormFieldConfig[] = [];
 for (const row of rows) {
   if (!isRecord(row) || typeof row.key !== 'string' ...) { warn; continue; }
   // duplicates
   if (seen.has(row.key)) { warn; continue; }
   seen.add(row.key);
   mapped.push({...});
 }
 return mapped.sort((a,b) => a.order - b.order || a.id - b.id);
}
```
Need de-dupe by key keeping lowest id. If rows unsorted by id, processing order may not be lowest id. Implement group:
- First filter/mapped valid rows (with options sanitized).
- Then sort by key then id asc, then keep first per key.
- Then sort by order asc, id asc.
Better.

Validation of DTOs: `FormFieldDto` typed but runtime may be malformed. Need defensive checks without `any`. Use `unknown` + type guards. Could write `isRecord(value: unknown): value is Record<string, unknown>`; then read fields via bracket. Since `noPropertyAccessFromIndexSignature`, must use bracket. Then build typed DTO:
```ts
private toFieldConfig(raw: unknown, country: CountryCode): FormFieldConfig | null {
 if (!isRecord(raw)) return null;
 const id = raw['id']; const documentId = raw['documentId']; const key = raw['key']; ...
 if (typeof id !== 'number' || typeof documentId !== 'string' || ... ) return null;
 if (!FIELD_TYPES.includes(type) ... ) ...
 const validations = isRecord(raw['validations']) ? raw['validations'] as ... : {};
```
Need parse validations safely with typed keys. Could implement `parseValidations(raw: unknown): FormFieldValidationRules` by reading known keys with `typeof` checks and allowed rule strings. Avoid casting to specific type from unknown with `as` maybe acceptable but strict "zero any" — `as` ok. But better explicit:
```ts
const result: Record<string, unknown> = {}; // then build object literal with number checks
```
Simpler use helper:
```ts
private parseValidations(raw: unknown): FormFieldValidationRules {
 if (!isRecord(raw)) return {};
 const rules: FormFieldValidationRules = {};
 const numbers = ['minLength','maxLength','min','max'] as const;
 ...
```
But `FormFieldValidationRules` has readonly props; assigning in function via object construction:
```
const rules: {
 minLength?: number; ...} = {};
if (typeof raw['minLength'] === 'number') rules.minLength = ...
```
Then return `rules as FormFieldValidationRules`? Since structure matches, TS allows assignment if object literal typed `FormFieldValidationRules` and we build mutable version. Define local mutable type:
```ts
type MutableValidations = { -readonly [K in keyof FormFieldValidationRules]?: FormFieldValidationRules[K] };
```
Then build and return as FormFieldValidationRules (compatible structurally since readonly props accept mutable). Fine.

Options: `parseOptions(raw, fieldKey)`: array of records with string value & label; drop malformed with dev warning. If type select and no options -> empty, select placeholder only.

`country` from DTO may mismatch? Use `country` from query param for domain config (defense in depth). Could warn if dto.country !== country and skip? Better skip rows with country mismatch to prevent leakage. But tests may mock DTO with country 'CO' while querying 'CO'. Fine. If mock omits country? User spec DTO includes country. Keep check: if `typeof raw['country'] === 'string' && raw['country'] !== country` skip + warn; if missing, assign requested country (to tolerate mocks). Good.

`getCountryConfig(country)`: URL exact `${apiBaseUrl}/api/country-configs?filters[country][$eq]=${country}`. Map `data[0] ?? null` with type guard; map `localeCode` string to AppLocale if valid (fallback catalog locale maybe), documentMaskVisibleEnd null → 4. `termsAndConditions` string fallback ''. Validate country. Check `isDevMode()` for warns. `environment` not needed.

Only file with `/api/` literal: our code uses `/api/` here only. Note `API_BASE_URL` token file imports environment (no /api/). Good.

### API token

`core/tokens/api-base-url.token.ts`:
```ts
import { InjectionToken } from '@angular/core';
import { environment } from '../../../environments/environment';
export const API_BASE_URL = new InjectionToken<string>('API_BASE_URL', {
  providedIn: 'root',
  factory: () => environment.strapiBaseUrl,
});
```
User says provided via environment.strapiBaseUrl; can also include in app.config providers explicitly `{ provide: API_BASE_URL, useValue: environment.strapiBaseUrl }`. To be safe with tests providing their own, do both? If token has factory and app.config provides explicit, fine. Include explicit provider in app.config importing environment. Actually user says "Create API_BASE_URL InjectionToken<string> provided via environment.strapiBaseUrl" — explicit provider in app.config is clear. Do both? Having factory default plus explicit provider harmless. But tests might override. Fine. I'll provide in appConfig: `{ provide: API_BASE_URL, useValue: environment.strapiBaseUrl }`.

### Storage

`core/persistence/storage.port.ts`:
```ts
export interface StoragePort {
  getItem(key: string): string | null;
  setItem(key: string, value: string): void;
  removeItem(key: string): void;
}
export const STORAGE_PORT = new InjectionToken<StoragePort>('STORAGE_PORT', {
  providedIn: 'root',
  factory: () => ({
    getItem: (key) => { try { return globalThis.sessionStorage?.getItem(key) ?? null; } catch { return null; } },
    ...
  })
});
```
Need avoid SSR? Browser only. Use `inject(DOCUMENT)`? Simpler `globalThis.sessionStorage`, with try/catch inside service too. To allow unit test fake throwing, service wraps calls.

Could make factory use `sessionStorage` directly inside try:
```ts
factory: () => ({
  getItem: (key: string): string | null => sessionStorage.getItem(key),
...
})
```
If sessionStorage undefined in tests, reference throws ReferenceError but inside service try/catch catches? `try { this.storage.getItem(...) } catch {}` — calling arrow that references undefined sessionStorage throws ReferenceError inside try, caught. Good. Use `globalThis.sessionStorage` to be safe.

`WizardPersistenceService`:
- key builder `storageKey(country)` exported maybe `wizardStorageKey`.
- TTL constant `DRAFT_TTL_MS = 2 * 60 * 60 * 1000` exported.
- `load(country)`:
```
try {
 const raw = storage.getItem(key);
 if (!raw) return null;
 const parsed: unknown = JSON.parse(raw);
 if (!isPersistedState(parsed)) { warn; storage.removeItem(key); return null; }
 if (parsed.schemaVersion !== 1 || parsed.country !== country) { remove; return null; }
 const savedAt = Date.parse(parsed.savedAt);
 if (!Number.isFinite(savedAt) || Date.now() - savedAt > TTL) { remove; return null; }
 return parsed;
} catch { dev warn; return null; }
```
Need also negative savedAt future? Could treat > now+some tolerance as invalid; not required. Add `savedAt > Date.now()+60000` invalid maybe. Keep simple.

- `save(state)`: try JSON.stringify, setItem. No errors.
- `clear(country)`: try remove.

`isPersistedState` type guard checks schemaVersion === 1, country in catalog, savedAt string, step valid, step1 record of FormValue, selectedPlanDocumentId string|null.

### DynamicFormFactory

Need implement typed controls and validators.

Type `WizardFormGroup = FormGroup<Record<string, FormControl<FormValue>>>;`

`createForm(fields, initial)`:
```
const controls: Record<string, FormControl<FormValue>> = {};
for (const field of fields) controls[field.key] = this.createControl(field, initial[field.key] ?? this.defaultValue(field));
const form = new FormGroup<Record<string, FormControl<FormValue>>>(controls);
this.applyCrossFieldValidators(form, fields);
form.updateValueAndValidity({ emitEvent: false });
return form;
```
Note `new FormGroup<Record<string, FormControl<FormValue>>>(controls)` typing: Angular's `FormGroup<TControl extends {...}>`, `Record<string, FormControl<FormValue>>` extends `{ [K in keyof TControl]: AbstractControl<any,any> }`? Should be fine. Could need `as` cast? Let's verify by build.

`createControl(field, initial)`:
- ensure initial coerced: `const value = coerceDraftValue(field, initial)`;
- validators array `ValidatorFn[]`:
  - required: checkbox → Validators.requiredTrue else Validators.required
  - minLength/maxLength if numbers
  - min/max if numbers and `field.validations.rule !== 'vehicleYear'`
  - pattern if string (try? `Validators.pattern(pattern)` with invalid regex might throw; Strapi config could be invalid. Wrap in try/catch with dev warn)
  - rule-specific:
    - legalAge: dateBoundsValidator + legalAgeValidator
    - vehicleYear: vehicleYearValidator(today) (integer + bounds)
    - clRut: clRutValidator
    - coPlate: plate validator using CO regex? rely on pattern + `normalizedPlateMatches(pattern)`; implement `patternOnNormalizedPlateValidator(field.validations.pattern ?? ...)`
    - clPlate similar
    - coDocumentNumber: control-level validator? Spec says group-level validator reading sibling `documentType`. To make unit tests of `createControl` see invalid when missing type, we may attach both a control-level validator that is a placeholder true and group validator. But group-level validators don't affect control's `.invalid`? In Angular, group validator errors appear on group, not control. Spec E04 expects 8 error paragraphs for CO empty form: required docNumber error yes via required; group-level error adds group error but field error still from required. For conditional rule with wrong length, control-level static minLength/pattern catches some, but CC vs CE difference (11 digits: CC invalid, CE valid) needs group validator. Need reflect error on specific control for UI. Approach: group-level validator sets error on the documentNumber control via `control.setErrors({ ...control.errors, coDocumentNumber: true })`? Angular validators should not mutate sibling ideally, but can. Better: implement group validator that attaches a custom error to the group AND sets `control.setErrors` on documentNumber. However `setErrors` inside group validator may cause recursion/infinite update? Angular calls group.validator after children update; `setErrors` on child doesn't trigger parent validation again unless emitEvent; should be okay if `{ emitEvent: false }`, but `setErrors` itself calls `_updateControlsValidity`? Let's avoid complexity.

Alternative: attach control-level validator closure with access to sibling via control.parent:
```
const coDocumentValidator: ValidatorFn = (control) => {
 const parent = control.parent;
 if (!parent) return null; // group validator will handle later
 const docTypeControl = parent.get('documentType');
 const documentType = docTypeControl ? String(docTypeControl.value ?? '') : '';
 const value = control.value;
 if (value === null || value === '') return null; // required handles
 return isValidCoDocument(documentType, String(value)) ? null : { coDocumentNumber: true };
};
```
Since Angular revalidates control when sibling changes? No, control validators only run when control value changes. Need group-level validator to trigger revalidation of that control when `documentType` changes. So implement group validator:
```
const validator: ValidatorFn = (group) => {
  const docControl = group.get('documentNumber');
  if (!docControl) return null;
  docControl.updateValueAndValidity({ onlySelf: true, emitEvent: false }); // careful recursion
  const err = docControl.errors?.['coDocumentNumber'] ? { coDocumentNumber: true } : null;
  return err;
};
```
Calling `updateValueAndValidity` on child within parent's validator might recurse: parent validation runs after child, child update with onlySelf true won't call parent, so okay. But Angular's parent `updateValueAndValidity` calls `this.validator(this)` after `_updateControlsValidity`; inside validator child.updateValueAndValidity(onlySelf true) recomputes child errors; no parent call. Fine. To avoid infinite loops, guard with flag. Use `let validating = false;`.

Actually cleaner: group validator computes error and calls `docControl.setErrors({ ...docControl.errors, coDocumentNumber: true })` with guard; but errors may be reset on next control validation. Let's use updateValueAndValidity approach with reentrancy guard.

Need when `documentType` missing: docNumber invalid. Control-level validator returns `{ coDocumentNumber: true }` if documentType empty and value non-empty. If both empty, required error on docNumber and documentType; group validator will also set coDocumentNumber on docNumber. Fine.

- `coerceDraftValue(field, rawValue)` exported:
```
if (rawValue === null || rawValue === undefined) return defaultValue(field);
switch (field.type) {
 case 'number': { const n = typeof raw === 'number' ? raw : Number(raw); return Number.isFinite(n) ? n : null; }
 case 'checkbox': return raw === true; // string "true" → false per spec
 case 'date':
 case 'select':
 case 'text': return typeof raw === 'string' ? raw : String(raw); // spec says String(value) if not null for others
}
```
But for select with `false` string "false"? fine. For number field default when invalid: spec says `null` (number → Number(value) if finite else null). However `defaultValue(field)` for number likely `''`? Spec says safe default `'' / null / false`; number field probably `null`. Use `''` for text/select/date, `null` for number, `false` for checkbox. Angular input type number with value null fine.

Potential issue `Validators.required` treats null and '' invalid, good.

- `defaultValue(field)`.

- `revalidate(form, fields)`: could rebuild validators on existing controls:
```
for (const field of fields) {
 const control = form.controls[field.key];
 if (!control) continue;
 control.setValidators(this.validatorsFor(field));
 control.updateValueAndValidity({ onlySelf: true, emitEvent: false });
}
form.clearValidators(); form.setValidators(this.groupValidators(fields)); form.updateValueAndValidity({ emitEvent: false });
```
Not strictly needed since createForm called fresh, but implement.

- `normalizeOnBlur(field, value)` maybe exported helper `normalizeFieldValue(field, value)`: applies `transform` and domain normalization:
  - if rule clRut → normalizeClRut
  - if rule coPlate/clPlate → normalizePlate
  - transform 'uppercase' → uppercase, 'trim' → trim; apply both if configured.
Need ensure blur in DynamicField uses it.

### WizardStore

Key requirements:
- signals fields, countryConfig, status, errorMessage, form, submitted, restoredFromDraft, isReady, errorCount.
- load(country) reconciliation; debounced 400ms auto-persist; retry(); persist(step); reset(country).
- Need avoid RxJS subscription leaks. Use `Subscription` field and unsubscribe on new load/reset; `DestroyRef` cleanup.
- `load(country)`:
```
this.status.set('loading'); this.errorMessage.set(null); this.submitted.set(false); this.restoredFromDraft.set(false);
this.catalogueSubscription?.unsubscribe();
try {
 const [fields, config] = await firstValueFrom(forkJoin({ fields: ..., config: ... }));
 this.fields.set(fields);
 this.countryConfig.set(config);
 if (fields.length === 0) { this.status.set('empty'); this.form.set(null); return; }
 const draft = this.persistence.load(country);
 const reconciled = this.reconcile(fields, draft?.step1);
 const form = this.factory.createForm(fields, reconciled);
 this.form.set(form);
 if (draft && Object.keys(draft.step1).length > 0) {
   this.restoredFromDraft.set(true);
   form.markAllAsTouched();
 }
 form.updateValueAndValidity({ emitEvent: false });
 this.status.set('ready');
 this.catalogueSubscription = form.valueChanges.pipe(debounceTime(400)).subscribe(() => this.persist('policyholder'));
} catch (error) {
 this.errorMessage.set(this.describeError(error));
 this.status.set('error');
